H 66128
	A large pocket within the nebula containing Silver-bearing asteroids and substantial quantities of fuel-rich gas. Krüger Minerals mines large quantities of Silver and processes it at their nearby Elbich Mining Facility. Red Hessian attacks are a constant danger, though, and transports must be well-protected.

H 66129
	DIAMETER: 12,762 km
	MASS: 5.87 x 10\f09²\4\F kg
	TERRAIN: Barren
	TEMPERATURE: -87°C to -12°C
	ESCAPE VELOCITY: 12.72 km/s
	
	A rocky planetoid devoid of any atmosphere. Initial surveys indicated an unusually large nickel core, but no other exploitable resources have been detected.

H 66130
	DIAMETER: 8,972 km
	MASS: 4.78 x 10\f09²\4\F kg
	TERRAIN: Desert
	TEMPERATURE: -70°C to 50°C
	ESCAPE VELOCITY: 9.75 km/s
	
	Despite possessing a thin atmosphere, this planetoid is rendered uninhabitable by the choking, acrid dust that covers its surface — in some places to a depth of several kilometers. All survey probes have either been swallowed by the dust oceans or ceased functioning shortly after planetfall, presumably due to the nearly microscopic dust particles working their way into the delicate electronics.

H 66131
	DIAMETER: 21,891 km
	MASS: 7.32 x 10\f09²\4\F kg
	TERRAIN: Terrestrial
	TEMPERATURE: 4°C to 39°C
	ESCAPE VELOCITY: 16.23 km/s
	
	\b\cr>>>RESTRICTED<<<\B\C
	
	An oddity in the annals of human exploration. While initial surveys indicated a seemingly idyllic world of thick forests and low fogs, the first expeditions to make planetfall quickly learned that an essential component of the planetary ecology was a fast-acting organic poison fatal to human beings. Every plant and animal examined on the planet contained this poison to one degree or another and any plans for settlement were quickly abandoned.

H 66132
	DIAMETER: 8,500 km
	MASS: 4.74 x 10\f09²\4\F kg
	TERRAIN: Barren
	TEMPERATURE: -75°C to -34°C
	ESCAPE VELOCITY: 9.72 km/s
	
	A rocky, airless moon, pockmarked by asteroid impact craters.

H 66133
	DIAMETER: 8,830 km
	MASS: 5.23 x 10\f09²\4\F kg
	TERRAIN: Volcanic
	TEMPERATURE: -23°C to 104°C
	ESCAPE VELOCITY: 10.23 km/s
	
	An active volcanic moon covered by large lakes of magma that flow from the moon’s molten core.

H 66134
	DIAMETER: 21,302 km
	MASS: 9.25 x 10\f09²\4\F kg
	TERRAIN: Barren
	TEMPERATURE: -100°C to -6°C
	ESCAPE VELOCITY: 21.24 km/s
	
	An incredibly dense planet that may have once been the core of a larger gas giant before it suffered some unknown cataclysm. While initial surveys located deposits of useful resources, the lack of any atmosphere and the expense of climbing out of the planetary gravity well made further development seem untenable.

H 66135
	DIAMETER: 17,322 km
	MASS: 5.09 x 10\f09²\4\F kg
	TERRAIN: Barren
	TEMPERATURE: -84°C to 66°C
	ESCAPE VELOCITY: 14.39 km/s
	
	An arid world whose atmospheric composition does not block harmful cosmic radiation, effectively rendering the planet sterile.

H 66136
	DIAMETER: 6,322 km
	MASS: 3.97 x 10\f09²\4\F kg
	TERRAIN: Ice
	TEMPERATURE: -123°C to -73°C
	ESCAPE VELOCITY: 7.23 km/s
	
	A small, icy moon whose low planetary mass was insufficient to keep its atmosphere from bleeding into space over millions of years.

H 66137
	DIAMETER: 16,902 km
	MASS: 5.91 x 10\f09²\4\F kg
	TERRAIN: Water
	TEMPERATURE: 3°C to 33°C
	ESCAPE VELOCITY: 15.61 km/s
	
	A planet largely covered in water. Unfortunately, an atmosphere composed of mostly non-reactive noble gases apparently inhibited any organic evolution, rendering its oceans for all intents and purposes — dead. The location of the planet has made any future prospect of terraforming unlikely at best.

H 66138
	DIAMETER: 11,321 km
	MASS: 5.11 x 10\f09²\4\F kg
	TERRAIN: Barren
	TEMPERATURE: -88°C to -46°C
	ESCAPE VELOCITY: 12.81 km/s
	
	A medium-sized world of swirling methane clouds locked in a gravitational embrace with its twin, Hades. Scientists have been unable to determine how two similar planets reached their present configuration.

H 66139
	DIAMETER: 12,190 km
	MASS: 5.12 x 10\f09²\4\F kg
	TERRAIN: Barren
	TEMPERATURE: -86°C to -42°C
	ESCAPE VELOCITY: 12.83 km/s
	
	A medium-sized world of swirling methane clouds locked in a gravitational embrace with its twin, Cerebus. Scientists have been unable to determine how two planets so similar to each other reached their present configuration.

H 66140
	Asteroid miners are manned or automated ships that extract valuable mineral deposits from asteroid fields.

H 66141
	\b\cr>>>WARNING: Do not attempt planetary landing without using a Docking Ring.<<<\B\C
	
	A Docking Ring is part of an orbital elevator system invented by Ageira Technologies. Through the use of super strong crystalline polymers and frictionless bearings, Docking Rings assist the smooth atmospheric entry and exit of interstellar vessels with landing capability.

H 66142
	A gas collector is an automated facility for capturing gases and dust for later refinement.

H 66143
	A gas miner is a station with limited mobility capable of extracting valuable resources from the gases found in nebulae.

H 66144
	A hazard buoy, used to indicate a region of potential danger for ships and their crew.

H 66145
	A Jump Gate allows instantaneous travel between two distinct points in space. Jump Gates were pioneered by Ageira Technologies and are constructed in partnership with Deep Space Engineering.

H 66146
	\b\cr***GRAVITATIONAL ANOMALY DETECTED***\B\C
	
	Magnetic and gravimetric readings are consistent with a Jump Hole — a natural phenomenon that functions similar to a Jump Gate, though the hazards associated with traveling through a Jump Hole remain largely unknown. Ageira Technologies recommends avoiding all contact with Jump Holes.

H 66147
	A navigational buoy, used for demarcating areas of high traffic.

H 66148
	A satellite, used to relay communications through deep space.

H 66149
	A solar plant is an automated facility for the collection, conversion, and storage of solar power from a nearby sun (or suns).

H 66150
	A storage depot typically houses a wide range of commodities in preparation for transport.

